  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 410 AM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nA near zonal to slightly northwest upper level flow remains over\nthe area today and Wednesday, followed by a transition to a\nbroad southwest upper level flow through the end of the work\nweek.\n\nCurrently, late evening into early overnight convection has\nended over the area. A few showers were noted over southeast \nMontana, otherwise skies were clear to partly cloudy. Low VFR to\nMVFR ceilings have been developing over the past couple of hours\nacross central and into northeast ND. RAP forecast 925-850mb RH\nindicates higher RH remaining along and south of the I-94\ncorridor through the morning and possibly into early afternoon.\nAs of yet there is little indication of any fog, but the\npotential will remain through mid-morning.\n\nThis afternoon will bring another chance of thunderstorms to\nwestern and central ND. In general, most guidance develops\nconvection in the late afternoon (after 21 UTC) over southwest \nND and tracks it southeast through the evening. There are some \nCAMS that extend convection farther north into northwest and \nnorth central ND, and others indicate late afternoon into \nevening convection, followed by another round of overnight \nconvection, possibly lingering into Wednesday morning. \n\nStrong (40-60kts) bulk shear will be over the forecast area \nthrough the period. The greatest instability will be in the\nsouthwest this afternoon into early this evening. The RAP does\nindicate some elevated instability remaining over western ND\nlate tonight with a weak inverted trough near the Montana\nborder. Thus the probability of a couple rounds of convection\ncan not be ruled out. However, there isn't much of a llj\novernight tonight which would help to maintain any late night\nconvection. The higher probability for strong to severe storms\nlooks to be late afternoon into this evening in the southwest\nand south central. With the strong shear and supercell wind\nprofiles, we will keep the previous hazards of golf ball sized\nhail and 70 mph winds. As there was earlier this evening, there\nwill be the potential for splitting cells again today. \n\nA similar setup on Wednesday will take place with the greatest\ninstability by afternoon in southwest/south central portion of\nthe state. Strong shear remains over the area so any storms that\nwould be able to develop sustain themselves, would have the\npotential to become severe. SPC currently has a marginal risk\nfor severe storms along and south of the I-94 corridor.\nCurrently, the instability does not look to be as strong\nWednesday so for the time being, will keep main hazards of\nquarter size hail and 60 mph winds. \n\nLater in the workweek, we transition to more of a broad\nsouthwest upper flow with a series of waves moving through the\nmean flow. With cooler temperatures in store Thursday and\nFriday, instability will remain low, thus we expect more \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms with a low threat for severe \nstorms.\n\nAfter Friday we begin a slow warm-up back to near normal\ntemperatures through the weekend and into early next week. \nWesterlies remain over the area so we would expect a \ncontinuation of the near daily chances for thunderstorms, rather \nthan a big warmup with dry conditions.",
